    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.minimum_cut_value - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.minimum_cut_value.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.minimum_cut_value()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Get minimum cut between source and sink nodes.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.minimum_cut_value()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Get minimum cut between source and sink nodes.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.minimum_cut_value()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Get minimum cut between source and sink nodes.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nGet minimum cut between source and sink nodes.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L73707-L73814){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.minimum_cut_value(\n    source,\n    target,\n    capacity=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Minimum cut between two nodes](../assets/graphs/minimum-cut-value.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Two diamonds that share the hub node D, with B and E outlined as source and sink. The two accent edges B–D and C–D are a smallest set of edges whose removal separates B from E. The card shows that cut and its size 2.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nComputes the minimum number of edges that must be removed to disconnect the source from the\nsink. Uses the Ford-Fulkerson algorithm with BFS (Edmonds-Karp) to find maximum flow, which\nequals the minimum cut by the max-flow min-cut theorem. Equivalently, the result is the\nmaximum number of edge-disjoint paths from source to sink.\n\nBy default every edge has capacity `1`, even on weighted graphs, so the answer is a count of\nedges; parallel edges count separately. With `capacity=` the answer is instead the total\ncapacity of the cheapest cut, as in `minimum_cut()`. For undirected graphs, edges are\ntreated as bidirectional. For directed graphs, only paths that follow edge direction from\n`source=` to `target=` count, so swapping the two can change the answer.\n\nUse this to measure how many independent links connect two specific nodes, for example\nhow many cable failures two data centers can tolerate before losing contact. If the\nnodes are already disconnected the result is `0`. To get the edges themselves, use\n`minimum_edge_cut()`; for the minimum over all pairs, use `edge_connectivity()`. The\nmethod does not modify the graph.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The node on the source side of the cut, given as an integer node ID or a string\n    label.\n\n<code>[target]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The node on the sink side of the cut, given as an integer node ID or a string label.\n    Must be a different node than `source=`.\n\n<code>[capacity]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Name of an edge attribute to use as capacity (`\"weight\"` also accepts the edge weight\n    of a weighted graph); every edge must hold a numeric value for it. If `None`\n    (default), every edge has capacity `1`.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [int | float]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Size of the minimum cut: the number of edges to remove (`int`), or with `capacity=`\n    their total capacity (`float`).\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If source or sink node doesn't exist, if they're the same, or if an edge lacks the\n    attribute named by `capacity=`.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~minimum_edge_cut`) \n\n:   The edges of the cut.\n\n[](`~minimum_cut`) \n\n:   The two sides of the cut.\n\n[](`~edge_disjoint_paths`) \n\n:   The paths whose number the cut equals.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nIn a chain `A - B - C - D` every edge is a bottleneck, so the minimum cut between the two\nends is 1. Closing the chain into a cycle adds a second, independent route:\n\n::: {#e69094c2 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\nchain = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\")])\nbefore = chain.minimum_cut_value(\"A\", \"D\")\nchain.add_edge(\"D\", \"A\")\n\nbefore, chain.minimum_cut_value(\"A\", \"D\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(1, 2)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nOn a directed graph the cut depends on direction. In the chain `A -> B -> C`, one edge\nseparates `A` from `C`, while `C` cannot reach `A` at all, giving a cut of `0`:\n\n::: {#10cc8602 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nchain = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\")], directed=True)\n\nchain.minimum_cut_value(\"A\", \"C\"), chain.minimum_cut_value(\"C\", \"A\")\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(1, 0)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Pipes or capacity\n\n`pipeline()` is a made-up city's water supply, with each pipe's `capacity` in megaliters\na day. Two pipes are enough to cut the reservoir off from the city, but with\n`capacity=\"capacity\"`, the cheapest cut is the one with the least capacity, 45 ML a\nday, the most the network can deliver:\n\n::: {#c7fc32d0 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npipeline = ec.pipeline()\n\n(\n    pipeline.minimum_cut_value(\"Reservoir\", \"City\"),\n    pipeline.minimum_cut_value(\"Reservoir\", \"City\", capacity=\"capacity\"),\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(2, 45.0)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#2ac35655 .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npipeline.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", label_by=\"label\", edge_label_by=\"capacity\",\n    width=700, height=360,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de